%erg 0.1
Title: erg-pr-merge dies on transient UNKNOWN mergeability at pre-flight though it already owns the settle poll
Created: 2026-09-10
Author: Minh Ha-Duong

--- log ---
2026-09-10T10:30Z Minh Ha-Duong created

--- body ---
## Context

Observed 2026-09-10 on PR #866. `erg-pr-merge` bounced with

    erg-pr-merge: mergeability is UNKNOWN — resolve conflicts or try again in a moment

and the PR did not merge. A re-run seconds later merged it cleanly, with not one
byte of the PR changed in between. The UNKNOWN was transient.

The script already knows this class of transient and already owns the cure.
`settle_mergeable()` polls until GitHub finishes recomputing, bounded by
`ERG_PR_MERGE_POLL_TRIES` (default 5), and its own comment says the value
"flips to UNKNOWN transiently" (ticket 0200). It is called **once**, on the
post-push recompute path.

The pre-flight check does not use it. It reads `.mergeable` out of the single
`gh pr view` snapshot taken at startup and dies on anything but `MERGEABLE`:

- read at `skills/merge/erg-pr-merge:109`
- `die` at `skills/merge/erg-pr-merge:111`
- `settle_mergeable()` defined at `:305`, called only at `:344`

GitHub computes mergeability asynchronously, so a PR opened or pushed moments
earlier legitimately reports UNKNOWN. That is exactly the situation an automated
caller creates: open a PR, wait for checks, merge. The faster the caller, the
more likely the bounce.

Why it stayed unnoticed is worth recording, because it is not part of this bug:
the calling session had piped the script into `tail -6`, and without `pipefail`
a pipeline reports the exit status of its last element, so `die`'s `exit 1`
surfaced as 0. The script's exit code is correct. Do not "fix" that here.

## Relevant files

- `skills/merge/erg-pr-merge` — `:109-111` the un-settled pre-flight check;
  `:294` the poll knobs; `:302-330` `settle_mergeable()`; `:344` its only call.
- `tests/` — no test currently exercises the pre-flight mergeability branch;
  grep for a literal from the die string rather than for the script name.

## Actions

1. Route the pre-flight check through `settle_mergeable()` instead of reading
   the startup snapshot: on a non-`MERGEABLE` first read, settle, then decide on
   the settled value.
2. Keep the failure loud and bounded. A genuinely stuck `UNKNOWN`, and a real
   `CONFLICTING`, must still `die` with the same message and exit 1. The bug is
   the absence of a retry, not the presence of a refusal — do not convert the
   check into a warning, and do not let the poll run unbounded.
3. `CONFLICTING` should not be polled at all: it is a settled answer, and
   polling it wastes the bounded budget. Only `UNKNOWN` deserves the settle.

## Test

Red first. `tests/test_erg_pr_merge_preflight_settles.sh`: stub `gh` so that
`pr view --json ...mergeable` returns `UNKNOWN` on the first call and
`MERGEABLE` on the second, then assert the script proceeds past the pre-flight
rather than exiting 1. Prove it red against the current script before fixing.
A second case must stay red-to-green in the other direction: a stub returning
`CONFLICTING` on every call must still exit 1, and must not consume the poll
budget.

## Verification

- [ ] First-read `UNKNOWN` followed by `MERGEABLE` proceeds to merge.
- [ ] Persistent `UNKNOWN` still exits 1, after at most
      `ERG_PR_MERGE_POLL_TRIES` polls.
- [ ] `CONFLICTING` exits 1 immediately, with no polling.
- [ ] The die message is unchanged, so callers matching on it keep working.
- [ ] `make check` passes.

## Invariants

- The script must never merge a PR whose settled mergeability is not
  `MERGEABLE`. This ticket removes a false negative; it must not create a false
  positive.
- Exit codes unchanged: 1 on refusal.
- No change to the post-push settle path, which works.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] A PR opened seconds before the merge call no longer bounces on a
      transient `UNKNOWN`.
- [ ] Both test cases above present and proved red against the pre-fix script.
- [ ] `settle_mergeable()` has two call sites and the pre-flight is one of them.
